I'll I'll re I'll reate my my question if you'd like me to um do you happen to know Mr Ridgeway when the the Wyman gaming commission um issues the the permits like you like you said you had to get a permit for this for this proposed facility um obviously the gaming commission probably has some kind of criteria to determine the feasibility yes or no on that permit so do you happen to know what the criteria uh is that the W gaming commission uses to determine saturation versus not saturation so I would answer this question based on what my experience has been going to every single gaming commission meeting for the last three and a half years um

I can't tell you what they do in the office themselves and how the how the board talks I can just talk about what seen at the public meetings so first of all we are required as part of our application to provide a feasibility study uh which is what we do we get through racing Resource Group is who we use uh the second thing that they do is they look at and Converse with the county itself um because if you've gotten a an approval from the local entity um then that tells you that the locality tends to believe that there's more room in the market and so they give some level of deference to that I've seen in the the past if they go well if the locality approved it then who are we to say um

the county or in this case the board of adjustment as well um because when we went in front of the gaming commission we already had a conditional use from the board of adjustment um then in addition what they would look at is they have historical data of how much tax revenue is being generated from these communities and what you're looking for when you're looking for saturation is is there a is does the line start to flatten at some point right when the line flattens when you have a new facility that's open that tells you that all that's happening is you have um you know an exchange between the existing operators as opposed to an expansion of a new market so

um as long as the tax revenue is continuing to go up meaning handle and takeouts going up then you haven't hit a saturation point so the other thing they would look at for our particular facility is uh information related to what they call win per unit so there are industry standards around um how much per machine is generated and then if those numbers are high for a facility then that tells you you have room for additional machines so in this particular case uh our wind per unit at at persing is pretty high for the industry which indicates we could add more machines but we have no space so they have to take into account the types of products that are offered by the different competitors too so

I believe all of that goes into it um the other thing I think is important is I'm I'm not aware of any other facilities after this one um that are in play in Cheyenne I mean I guess there is a fourth operator that wants to open a racetrack and I don't know what the gaming commission will do with that or what will happen with locations in this County based on how people analyze saturation uh I think part of the reason we were approved is because we'd been working on this transaction since late fall 2023 and it just takes that long to get permitted um but there's not much pending in terms of new locations in this County that I'm aware of I don't know that there's any that have a pending application or anywhere in the process at this point thank

